<Project Sdk="Microsoft.NET.Sdk">

  <PropertyGroup>
    <AllowUnsafeBlocks>true</AllowUnsafeBlocks>
    <TargetFramework>net10.0</TargetFramework>
    <Nullable>enable</Nullable>
    <ImplicitUsings>enable</ImplicitUsings>
    <RootNamespace>Serval.Ai</RootNamespace>
    <RuntimeIdentifiers>linux-x64;linux-arm64</RuntimeIdentifiers>
  </PropertyGroup>

  <ItemGroup>
    <InternalsVisibleTo Include="Serval.Ai.Tests" />
  </ItemGroup>

  <ItemGroup>
    <ProjectReference Include="../Serval.Ai.Core/Serval.Ai.Core.csproj" />
    <ProjectReference Include="../Serval.Contracts/Serval.Contracts.csproj" />
  </ItemGroup>

  <ItemGroup>
    <PackageReference Include="Microsoft.Extensions.Logging.Abstractions" Version="10.0.14" />
    <PackageReference Include="Microsoft.Extensions.Options" Version="10.0.14" />
  </ItemGroup>

  <!--
    Vision. LLamaSharp.Backend.Cpu carries llama.cpp + libmtmd for linux-x64 AND linux-arm64,
    so Qwen3-VL runs in-process on the desktop, the Pi, and the Server from one code path.

    Vulkan is opt-in via -p:LlamaBackend=Vulkan, which the Server's Dockerfile passes so the
    container can offload to an AMD/Intel GPU (see VisionOptions.GpuLayers). It is NOT the
    default: LLamaSharp.Backend.Vulkan.Linux ships linux-x64 only, so making it unconditional
    would break the linux-arm64 CameraModule.

    Vulkan does not replace Cpu, it adds to it — LLamaSharp.Backend.Vulkan.Linux takes a
    dependency on LLamaSharp.Backend.Cpu, so the publish carries both runtimes/linux-x64/native/
    vulkan/ and the avx* variants, and llama.cpp falls back to the CPU when no Vulkan device is
    usable. A container without /dev/dri therefore still runs, just on the CPU. That means the
    Cpu PackageReference below is redundant in the Vulkan configuration and only load-bearing
    for arm64 — the condition is kept for clarity about which build gets what.
  -->
  <PropertyGroup>
    <LlamaBackend Condition="'$(LlamaBackend)' == ''">Cpu</LlamaBackend>
  </PropertyGroup>

  <ItemGroup>
    <PackageReference Include="LLamaSharp" Version="0.30.0" />
    <PackageReference Include="LLamaSharp.Backend.Cpu" Version="0.30.0"
                     Condition="'$(LlamaBackend)' == 'Cpu'" />
    <PackageReference Include="LLamaSharp.Backend.Vulkan" Version="0.30.0"
                     Condition="'$(LlamaBackend)' == 'Vulkan'" />
  </ItemGroup>

  <!--
    JPEG decode. Two callers: the NPU vision path (RkllmVisionRunner), which must hand the RKNN
    encoder raw RGB, and the motion gate, which needs downscaled grayscale. See JpegDecoder.

    SkiaSharp is Microsoft's (MIT), and its JPEG codec is libjpeg-turbo, which decodes at 1/2, 1/4
    or 1/8 scale inside the IDCT — the motion gate's downscale costs almost nothing as a result.
    The NoDependencies native asset bundles libSkiaSharp for linux-x64 and linux-arm64 without the
    fontconfig dependency, so neither the Server's container nor the Pi needs anything installed.
    The two packages ship in lockstep and must carry the same version.

    Not ImageSharp: from 3.2 it fails the compile unless a Six Labors license key is present on the
    machine doing the build, and Serval is built from source by whoever clones it.
  -->
  <ItemGroup>
    <PackageReference Include="SkiaSharp" Version="4.154.0" />
    <PackageReference Include="SkiaSharp.NativeAssets.Linux.NoDependencies" Version="4.154.0" />
  </ItemGroup>

  <!--
    SenseVoice does ASR + speech emotion + audio events in one pass, on both
    linux-x64 and linux-arm64 (RK3588). Version is pinned exactly: SenseVoiceInterop
    mirrors the native SherpaOnnxOfflineRecognizerResult struct layout to reach the
    emotion field, which the managed wrapper does not expose. Bumping this version
    requires re-checking that struct against c-api.h.
  -->
  <ItemGroup>
    <PackageReference Include="org.k2fsa.sherpa.onnx" Version="[1.13.4]" />
    <PackageReference Include="org.k2fsa.sherpa.onnx.runtime.linux-x64" Version="[1.13.4]" Condition="'$(RuntimeIdentifier)' == 'linux-x64' Or '$(RuntimeIdentifier)' == ''" />
    <PackageReference Include="org.k2fsa.sherpa.onnx.runtime.linux-arm64" Version="[1.13.4]" Condition="'$(RuntimeIdentifier)' == 'linux-arm64'" />
  </ItemGroup>

  <!--
    Object detection. This is the *Managed* package — the managed P/Invoke surface with no native
    assets of its own — and that is load-bearing rather than incidental.

    The full Microsoft.ML.OnnxRuntime package publishes its native to
    runtimes/linux-x64/native/libonnxruntime.so, which is byte-for-byte where sherpa-onnx publishes
    its own. The SDK resolves that collision silently, in sherpa's favour or Microsoft's depending
    on ordering, with no error at any verbosity. Since libsherpa-onnx-c-api.so declares
    NEEDED libonnxruntime.so with RPATH $ORIGIN, losing that coin toss rebinds the entire ASR path
    onto a different ONNX Runtime build. Referencing Managed alone means exactly one native ships
    and DllImport("onnxruntime") resolves to it.

    So the detector runs on sherpa's ONNX Runtime 1.27.0 (Managed 1.27.1 is API-compatible with it),
    which ties the two together: bumping the sherpa pin above means re-checking this still loads,
    and it fixes the detector to the CPU execution provider, because that build carries no others.
    A GPU provider means a purpose-built libonnxruntime.so on the image and
    DetectionOptions.NativeLibraryPath pointing at it, not a different package here.

    Version is pinned exactly, for the same reason the sherpa pin below is.
  -->
  <ItemGroup>
    <PackageReference Include="Microsoft.ML.OnnxRuntime.Managed" Version="[1.27.1]" />
  </ItemGroup>

</Project>
